/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   A token resolving to 69 in a leading comment opens a nested comment and
   swallows the rest of the file - see RMSTEST_56 and 57. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>

  /* THE READING. The whole map takes one of three colours. */
  if PLAYER1_TEAM1
    base_terrain SNOW
  elseif PLAYER1_TEAM2
    base_terrain DESERT
  else
    base_terrain GRASS
  endif

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* THE SECOND READING, present or absent. A big dark blob in the middle,
     which cannot be confused with the base colour whichever one it is. */
  if TEAM1_SIZE1
    create_terrain WATER
    {
      base_terrain SNOW
      number_of_clumps 1
      number_of_tiles 3000
      clumping_factor 80
    }
    create_terrain WATER
    {
      base_terrain DESERT
      number_of_clumps 1
      number_of_tiles 3000
      clumping_factor 80
    }
    create_terrain WATER
    {
      base_terrain GRASS
      number_of_clumps 1
      number_of_tiles 3000
      clumping_factor 80
    }
  endif

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_44 - is canonical team numbering by lowest player number, or by
ascending selected team number?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.3.1 step 3, src/generationSettings/teamModel.ts,
       Sec.15 item 17(a)

THIS SCRIPT WAS REWRITTEN 2026-08-11 AFTER TWO WASTED RUNS AND ONE UNREADABLE
ONE. The question never changed; the way it is read did, and the reasons are
worth more than the answer.

  Attempt 1 - editor, object counts. Wasted: the lobby was the habitual
              odd-versus-even split, under which BOTH models predict the same
              label. A setup that cannot discriminate does not announce itself -
              it generates cleanly and returns a confident number.
  Attempt 2 - editor again. Wasted for a different reason: THE EDITOR CANNOT SET
              TEAM NUMBERS AT ALL. It sets who is allied with whom, and the
              alliance can even be one-sided. A team NUMBER is a lobby concept,
              and this question is entirely about which number wins.
  Attempt 3 - lobby, saved game. Unreadable: a saved game is not a scenario. It
              decompresses (raw deflate, ~14 MB) but it is game state, not the
              scenario format the probe reads. Byte-counting the object id in
              the decompressed blob is not a substitute - it returned 22 for an
              object placed exactly 25 times, so the error is the same size as
              the effect.

SO THE OBSERVABLE HAS TO BE VISIBLE IN A RUNNING GAME, WITH NO EXPORT AND NO
TOOL. That is what this rewrite is. There is nothing to save and nothing to
probe: start the game, look at the minimap, quit.

THE QUESTION
Sec.3.1 step 3 says the canonical team number comes from the LOWEST PLAYER
NUMBER on each team - the team holding the lowest-numbered player becomes team
1. teamModel.ts implements that. The rival reading is ASCENDING SELECTED NUMBER:
the team whose picked number is smallest becomes canonical 1, whoever is on it.

THE LOBBY. THIS IS THE WHOLE COST OF THE TEST AND IT HAS BEEN GOT WRONG TWICE.

  THE INVARIANT: the lowest-numbered PLAYER must not be on the lowest-numbered
  TEAM.

Four players:

  player 1 -> team 2
  player 2 -> team 2
  player 3 -> team 1
  player 4 -> team 1

The groupings are the habitual two-versus-two. Only the numbers are swapped, and
the numbers are the entire question. Left at the default, player 1 sits on team
1, both models agree, and the run says nothing again.

WHAT YOU WILL SEE

  WHITE map (snow)    -> player 1 is in canonical team 1 -> LOWEST PLAYER
                         NUMBER. Sec.3.1 stands, teamModel.ts is correct.
  YELLOW map (desert) -> player 1 is in canonical team 2 -> ASCENDING SELECTED
                         NUMBER. Sec.3.1 step 3 is wrong and teamModel.ts
                         mislabels the environment on every team game.
  GREEN map (grass)   -> NEITHER label is defined. Not a null result: it would
                         mean the labels do not fire the way both models assume
                         and the derivation needs rebuilding.

SECOND CONFIGURATION, AND IT IS NOT RESOLVED
Five players, player 1 ALONE on their own team, players 2 to 5 together.

  A large WATER blob in the middle -> the size-1 label fires, so a solo player
                                      DOES carry a team of one.
  NO blob                          -> it does not, agreeing with this project's
                                      "a team of one is not a team" rule.

An editor run on 2026-08-11 produced the equivalent of the blob - the label
fired - AND the equivalent of a green map, neither player-in-team label defined.
Those two together fit neither candidate model. But that run was made in the
EDITOR, which has no team numbers, so what it was testing is unclear and it
cannot stand as an answer. Run this configuration from the lobby too.

Both configurations read off the same two features: base colour, and blob.

GENERATE AT TINY (120) FROM A REAL GAME LOBBY. ONE GAME PER CONFIGURATION.

Nothing needs saving. If you want a record, a screenshot of the minimap is a
better artefact than a save file, for the reason attempt 3 records.

WHY THE BLOB IS WRITTEN THREE TIMES
create_terrain matches on a base terrain, and the base here is whichever of the
three the branch above chose. Three commands, one per possible base, so exactly
one matches and the blob appears regardless of which colour won. Writing it once
against a guessed base would make the second reading silently depend on the
first.
*/
